p.src:
The basic electromagnetic variable is the four-vector potential $A_\mu(x)$. The electric and magnetic fields will be derived from it. Along a small worldline segment $dx^\mu$, the contraction $A_\mu(x)\,dx^\mu$ is a scalar. Adding those scalar increments from one endpoint to the other gives a Lorentz-invariant interaction: $S_{\mathrm{int}}
=
-e\int_1^2 A_\mu(x)\,dx^\mu.$ The coefficient $e$ is the electric charge. The overall sign belongs to the historical charge convention; changing the convention changes the sign assigned to $e$, not the content of the theory.

